{# The Jev state, as a Jinja2 template.

   It renders to text and is then parsed as JSON, so every value that comes
   from an email goes through the `tojson` filter. That filter emits a fully
   quoted and escaped JSON value, which is why nothing here has quotes around
   it. Take the filter off and the first message containing a quote mark or a
   newline turns the whole state into invalid JSON.

   Available variables.

     thread.subject
     thread.sender
     thread.date
     thread.message_count
     thread.messages[]  with sender, recipient, date, body, has_attachments

   `has_attachments` is a plain boolean so a label can be asked about it. A
   receipts label that expects an attached invoice is the obvious case.
   Filenames are left out, because Jev cannot open an attachment and the names
   would be context spent for nothing.

   The thread ID and the snippet are left out for the same reason. Both exist
   on the server, they just are not worth paying for here.
#}
{
  "thread": {
    "subject": {{ thread.subject | tojson }},
    "from": {{ thread.sender | tojson }},
    "date": {{ thread.date | tojson }},
    "messages": [
{% for m in thread.messages %}
      {
        "from": {{ m.sender | tojson }},
        "date": {{ m.date | tojson }},
        "has_attachments": {{ m.has_attachments | tojson }},
        "text": {{ m.body | tojson }}
      }{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
    ]
  }
}
